	<article id="aboutme">
		<div id="aboutme_div">
				<h1>About me</h1>
				<div style="display: inline-block; margin-left: 1em; vertical-align: middle; padding-bottom: 0.6em;" class="github-profile-badge" data-user="clementtttttttt"></div>
		</div>
		<div class="art_content">
		<p>Hello. I'm Clement Poon, a Computer Science student at Queen Mary University of London, and I have a passion for embedded systems engineering.
		
		</p>
		<p>I started my programming journey back in 2020, when I took my first C++ course on codecademy. Shortly after I learnt 8086 assembly and wrote my very first "operating system" at the age of 13, named <a href="https://github.com/clementtttttttt/NetDOS">NetDOS</a>. It has since snowballed into an integral part of my identity.</p>
		<p>In my free time, I also work with low power electronics and modify cheap remote controlled cars as a hobby. I am also an amateur jazz pianist, and my current obession is the pianist Bill Evans. You can find out more about my jazz piano obsession <a href="#jazz">here.</a></p>
		</div>
		<h1>Skills</h1>

			<div class="art_content">

		<h2>C++, C (Mainly Linux), Java</h2>
		<p>
			Have been using them ever since the very start of my coding journey. Pretty proficient if I do say so myself.
		</p>
		<h2>x86_64, i386, 6502/6809 assembly</h2>
		<p>
			Picked them up when I was working on my Eagle-88 and Osprey-68 projects. Slight rant but I absolutely hate 8086 assembly, it's designed as a stopgap and it really shows. 6809 is the complete opposite, which ironically made it a bit of a commercial failure. 6502 assembly is basically the same as 6809, except without the fancy costly features such as 8-bit hardware multiplication and an additional register of course. 
		</p>
					
		<hr>
		</div>

	</article>

		<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/Rapsssito/github-profile-badge@latest/src/widget.min.js"></script>
	<article id="jazz">
		<h1>Why jazz?</h1>
		<div class="art_content">
			<h3>If you don't have the time to read, you can watch the videos.</h3>
			<figure id="bemusic">
			   <iframe loading="lazy" width=80% height=400em src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/uco5FNbjqv0?si=wcD5S0yyvE7c-9_T" title="YouTube video player" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen></iframe>
			   <iframe loading="lazy" width=80% height=400em src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/x5arK5GWXG4?si=ti3VlufPXBfUf58m" title="YouTube video player" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen></iframe>
		   </figure>
			<p>Classical music gets old because well, it's old. Most stuff like Mozart, Beethoven, Chopin, etc, while timeless, are performed with minimal variation. Pop music also gets old because most are rather repetitive, with a fixed 1-5-6-4 chord progression vamp making them appealing to the masses but not very interesting musically. Of course, there are notable exceptions such as Amy Winehouse who were successful in their innovations, which I very much do appreciate.  </p>
			<p>Jazz is all about making stuff on the fly, by using an existing foundation of chords, or just out of thin air. This makes every individual recording and rendition of a jazz standard unique. There is no way to perfectly replicate a specific jazz performance, without a transcription. </p>
			
			<section>
			<h2>Why Bill Evans?</h2>
			<p>Bill Evans <em>speaks</em> with the piano. There are many jazz players with impressive flashy technique that some consider surpasses Bill Evans, such as Art Tatum, Oscar Peterson, and Erroll Garner. Don't get me wrong, they are great players. But Bill Evans stands out from the rest by actually putting thought into each note, and chord. Through his compositional genius, every note he plays serves a musical purpose, and he is able to carefully build and release tension with them.<p>
			
			<h2>How well can you play?</h2>
			<p>Well enough for jam sessions. You can probably find me in several free jam sessions in London. Though currently I am still working on improving my left-hand comping and voice-leading technique. </p>
			</section>
		</div>
	
	
	
	</article>







